  "configuration_id": "config_047",
  "reason": "Evidence: The current best is config_020 (multimodal, multiprofile, pca_n2000, resnet50, avgpool, weighted; acc=0.639). We know pca_n2000 >> scgpt for RNA encoding, multiprofile > singleprofile, and weighted > concat fusion. However, the image encoder (resnet50) is the one major design axis not yet explored at the top-performing level—image alone scored only 0.208 with resnet50, suggesting the image branch may be underperforming. Question: Does upgrading the image encoder to imagenet_vit_l (config_047) improve over resnet50 while holding all other settings identical to config_020? This is a clean single-variable comparison that isolates the image-encoder effect. Why best next: It directly extends the strongest known configuration along the most impactful untested dimension. If ViT-L helps, we gain a new best and confirm image quality matters; if not, we confirm resnet50 is sufficient and can redirect budget to aggregation or other axes. This is more informative than testing transformer aggregation (where prior scgpt evidence suggests only a ~1.5% gap) or introducing an entirely new RNA encoder (scvi_n200) which would confound multiple variables."
}

